Subject: [PATCH 1/2] feat(desktop): opt-in Glass on Linux via a transparent
 chat window

Linux has no first-party Electron window material, but a compositor
(KWin, Hyprland, Mutter with ext-background-effect-v1, picom) blurs
whatever lies behind a transparent window. Launching with
HERMES_DESKTOP_LINUX_GLASS=1 or --linux-glass now reports Glass as
supported on Linux and creates chat windows with transparent: true
when Glass is active at launch.

It is opt-in because Glass is the default wherever it is supported,
and on a desktop that does not blur a transparent window is plainly
see-through. Without the opt-in nothing changes:
glassSupportedOn('linux') still returns false.

Transparency is fixed when a window is created, so switching Glass on
in Settings takes effect after a restart; a window born opaque keeps
its themed backing instead of being handed a clear colour.

Tested: electron/translucency.test.ts (82 passing, 4 new). Not yet run
on a Linux desktop.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
